{"name":"Renaissance Fresco","hex":"#ebc8b8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"renaissance","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rebirth or revival.","Alternative letter-case form of Renaissance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/renaissance"},{"term":"fresco","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cool, refreshing state of the air; coolness, duskiness, shade.","An artwork made by applying water-based pigment to wet or fresh lime mortar or plaster."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To paint using fresco."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fresco"}],"entries":[{"title":"Renaissance","blocks":[{"text":"The Renaissance was an European period of history and cultural movement taking place at the end of the Late Middle Ages and the beginning of the early modern era. It is variously defined as covering the 15th and 16th centuries or, more broadly, as lasting from the 14th century to the 17th. It was characterized by the European rediscovery and revival of the literary, philosophical, and artistic achievements of classical antiquity. Associated with great change in art, architecture, politics, literature, exploration and technology, the Renaissance was first centered in the Republic of Florence, then spread to the rest of Italy and later throughout Europe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Da_Vinci_Vitruve_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Da_Vinci_Vitruve_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Renaissance"},{"title":"Fresco","blocks":[{"text":"Fresco is a technique of mural painting executed upon freshly laid (\"wet\") lime plaster. Water is used as the vehicle for the dry-powder pigment to merge with the plaster, and with the setting of the plaster, the painting becomes an integral part of the wall. The word fresco is derived from the Italian adjective fresco meaning \"fresh\", and may thus be contrasted with fresco-secco or secco mural painting techniques, which are applied to dried plaster, to supplement painting in fresco. The fresco technique has been employed since antiquity and is closely associated with Italian Renaissance painting."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Creaci%C3%B3n_de_Ad%C3%A1n_%28Miguel_%C3%81ngel%29.jpg/330px-Creaci%C3%B3n_de_Ad%C3%A1n_%28Miguel_%C3%81ngel%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fresco"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_%28Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph%29_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of a Young Woman (c. 1480–85) (Simonetta Vespucci) by Sandro Botticelli","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASandro_Botticelli_-_Idealized_Portrait_of_a_Lady_(Portrait_of_Simonetta_Vespucci_as_Nymph)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org